WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.960
Speaker 1: There is evidence to criminally&nbsp;
charge the failed former president Donald Trump,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:15.540
says Adam Schiff, member of the January 6th&nbsp;
committee. We have video of Adam Schiff explaining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:21.480
I've seen the evidence. There is evidence that&nbsp;
Donald Trump committed criminal offenses in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:27.660
effort to overturn the 2020 election. Now,&nbsp;
Adam Schiff has previously said things like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:33.720
we have the evidence that will put Dan Junior&nbsp;
in prison or at least get him charged. And it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:37.680
appears as though he was wrong about&nbsp;
that. So Adam Schiff is no panacea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:43.500
but we are within hours right now of the January&nbsp;
six committee making criminal referrals. We don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.460
yet know who's going to be on that list. And&nbsp;
you have Adam Schiff saying the following.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:50.760
Let's take a listen and then discuss what we'll&nbsp;
discuss. Well, what could be those charges?&nbsp;

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:56.400
Speaker 2: I think that the evidence is there&nbsp;
that Donald Trump committed criminal offenses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:02.280
in connection with his efforts to overturn the&nbsp;
election and viewing it. As a former prosecutor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:08.520
I think there's sufficient evidence to charge&nbsp;
the president. Why? I don't know. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:12.720
I don't know what the Justice Department has. I&nbsp;
do know what's in the public record. The evidence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.520
seems pretty plain to me. But I would want&nbsp;
to see the full body of evidence if I were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:24.840
in the prosecutor's shoes to make a decision.&nbsp;
But, you know, this is someone who in multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:30.840
ways tried to pressure state officials to find&nbsp;
votes that didn't exist. This is someone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:36.540
tried to interfere with a joint session, even&nbsp;
inciting a mob to attack the Capitol. True.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:44.040
If that's not criminal, then. Not all it is.
Speaker 1: So let's take this seriously and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:49.860
think it through. Let's not speak in general terms&nbsp;
about this. According to the investigation, by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:55.080
January six committee, there is evidence that&nbsp;
Trump committed crimes. That's what Adam Schiff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.080 --> 00:02:00.660
is telling us. And we've heard it from others.&nbsp;
The committee did find that Trump made false&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:05.400
statements about the results of the 2020 election&nbsp;
in so far as he claimed that he won even though he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:11.640
lost. And then he used those false statements to&nbsp;
encourage his followers to challenge the results.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:19.140
In so doing, he encouraged the violent attack&nbsp;
on the US Capitol on January six, 2021,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:26.340
which about a million of us on our stream watched&nbsp;
live on that day. The evidence against Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:31.980
includes his own words, which the committee has&nbsp;
looked at extensively. The actions of those who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:36.600
participated in the attacks and their claims that&nbsp;
they were there because Trump told them to go.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:44.160
Trump made repeated claims that the election was&nbsp;
stolen from him. He made false claims that it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:51.660
stolen in particular ways none of which were found&nbsp;
to be have any merit. He urged his supporters to,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:57.060
quote, never give up, to never concede, to&nbsp;
fight like hell, to march to the Capitol,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:03.240
to show support for him. In addition to Trump's&nbsp;
public words, we have evidence of the planning and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:10.320
the coordination and the behind the scenes of this&nbsp;
plan that was drafted up for Trump, for Mike Pence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:14.280
to again, at this point, we don't even really&nbsp;
understand what he was supposed to do, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:18.600
send it back to the states or recognize alternate&nbsp;
electors, all the things that he couldn't do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:24.720
But there was planning, there was legal work that&nbsp;
went into that. And we have other individuals that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:29.700
were involved who were caught on video discussing&nbsp;
the plan, discussing how to disrupt the election&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:35.040
results. And some were even seeing wearing&nbsp;
tactical gear and some of them even had weapons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:42.420
So this was all a major plan. If Donald Trump&nbsp;
were to be charged in connection with the attack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:49.080
from what happened on January 6th, the possible&nbsp;
consequences could include fines, imprisonment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:52.680
or both. And we'll talk about the prison piece a&nbsp;
little later. Glenn Kershner had an interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:56.820
comment about that's all hypothetical,&nbsp;
but what would the specific charges be?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.820 --> 00:04:05.640
Trump could face potentially incitement to riot&nbsp;
conspiracy, obstruction of justice among other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:10.920
crimes. Each carries its own potential&nbsp;
penalties, and the penalties depend on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:15.540
the severity of the offense and other factors,&nbsp;
including what he would ultimately be convicted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:20.280
of in this completely hypothetical scenario we're&nbsp;
talking about now, of course. Just because you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:23.580
charged doesn't mean you're going to be found&nbsp;
guilty. In the interview with Adam Schiff. Jake&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:27.000
TAPPER asks a good question, which is, okay,&nbsp;
you might have the evidence for an indictment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:31.800
but you have the evidence for a conviction. Those&nbsp;
are two dramatically different questions. And at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:37.560
the end of the the entire thing is a question&nbsp;
I've asked before. Can you find a jury in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.560 --> 00:04:41.580
United States that will fairly adjudicate&nbsp;
Donald Trump? And what I mean by that is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:48.300
is there really anyone in the U.S. who would&nbsp;
say, I am open minded enough about Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:55.560
that whatever preconceived notions I have about&nbsp;
him and what he did, I will rule based only on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:59.580
the evidence presented in this trial. I don't know&nbsp;
that I could do that. I don't know that I could do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:06.180
that because it would be very difficult for me to&nbsp;
completely put aside everything that over the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.180 --> 00:05:11.760
five years and in the couple of years since the&nbsp;
riots in the year plus since the riot, almost two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.760 --> 00:05:16.740
years since the riots, I have learned about Donald&nbsp;
Trump. So so there is a real question, can Trump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:21.000
get an actual fair jury trial in the U.S.? That'll&nbsp;
be a conversation we have a little bit later.

